# Building and deploying {{ __node_setup_name }}

This directory was written by `ansible-playbook aslib.infra.node_setup`. Run every
command below **from this directory**, on a machine with ansible-core and the
aslib.infra collection installed (see aslib.infra's README). Every run writes its
output to a file of its own in `logs/`, such as `logs/deploy-20261003T081502Z.log`.

| File | What it is | Whose | In git |
|---|---|---|---|
| `inventory.yml` | The servers to deploy to | Yours | Yes |
| `group_vars/all/project.yml` | The project's settings: written with every default, then yours | Yours | Yes |
| `container.env` | The container's settings and secrets, first copied from `../.env.production` if the project has one | Yours | **No** |
| `group_vars/all/defaults.yml` | Every setting at aslib.infra's default; `project.yml` wins | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `prebuild.yml` | Installs node_modules for development, in a throwaway container | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `image.yml`, `deploy.yml`, `build-and-deploy.yml`, `remove.yml` | Build, deploy, both in one go, remove | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `templates/service.container.j2` | The Quadlet file: how Podman and systemd run the service | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `update-playbooks.yml`, `ansible.cfg`, `.gitignore`, this guide | | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `images/`, `logs/`, `*.bak` | Built images, the log of every run, copies an update keeps | | No |

Your files are never overwritten. aslib.infra's are brought up to date by every
update (step 5), which keeps the old version of each changed file as
`<file>.bak`: a change that would help every project belongs in aslib.infra
itself. Only this guide names the project; the other files take its name and
version from package.json whenever they run.

The steps, in the order a project goes through them:

1. [Set up](#1-set-up): this directory, and what to fill in
2. [Prebuild](#2-prebuild-node_modules-for-development): node_modules for development
3. [Build and deploy](#3-build-and-deploy): the image, the service, new versions
4. [Check on it](#4-check-on-it): the services on the servers
5. [Update aslib.infra](#5-update-aslibinfra): this directory, after a newer aslib.infra
6. [Remove it](#6-remove-it): the service, from the servers

## 1. Set up

`ansible-playbook aslib.infra.node_setup`, run from the project root, wrote this
directory. Before the first deploy, look at what is yours:

- `container.env`: the environment variables the app gets, one `KEY=value` per
  line, without quotes (Podman would pass them on). If it sets `PORT`, set
  `podman_service_container_port` to the same port.
- `../config/production/*.json`: the app's config files. The deploy copies them
  to the server, where the app finds them read-only in `config/`.
- `group_vars/all/project.yml`: the port on the server (`podman_service_port`),
  the service's directory, its account, the Node.js version and more.
- `templates/service.container.j2`: the Quadlet file, for what the settings do
  not cover. The deploy fills in the names and the version.
- `inventory.yml`: the servers. It lists this machine by default.

What the project needs: a `build` script that makes `dist/main.js`
(`node_image_main`), and optionally `.nvmrc` and `package-lock.json`. Everything
the app imports must be in `dependencies`, and not in `devDependencies` as well:
the image installs `devDependencies` for the build only, and npm counts a package
in both lists as a devDependency (the build stops when it finds one).

## 2. Prebuild: node_modules for development

```sh
ansible-playbook prebuild.yml
```

Installing dependencies runs code from every package (install scripts), so this
runs npm in a throwaway container of the image build's builder image instead of
on this machine. The container gets `package.json`, `package-lock.json` and
`.npmrc`, nothing else, and is removed afterwards. Only once the install has
succeeded, the current `node_modules` is saved as `node_modules.<UTC time>.bak.tgz`
and replaced. To go back:

```sh
cd .. && rm -rf node_modules && tar -xzf node_modules.<UTC time>.bak.tgz
```

The backups stay until you delete them; keep them out of git (`*.tgz` in the
project's `.gitignore`). The result fits Linux machines with the same Node.js
version as `.nvmrc`.

## 3. Build and deploy

```sh
ansible-playbook build-and-deploy.yml    # add --ask-become-pass if sudo asks for a password
```

This runs `image.yml`, then `deploy.yml`. Each also runs on its own: a change to
`container.env`, the config files, the settings or the Quadlet template alone
needs only `ansible-playbook deploy.yml`.

**The image** (`image.yml`) needs Podman 3.4 or newer (RHEL 9 or Ubuntu 22.04)
and access to the [download sources](#download-sources). It lands in `images/` as
`{{ __node_setup_service }}-<version>.tar`, with the version from package.json.
This directory, `config/`, `data/`, `*.db` and `.env` files never go into it.
This machine keeps two images of the project, `{{ __node_setup_image }}:<version>`
and `{{ __node_setup_image }}:build` (the cache); older ones are removed. All
options: `ansible-doc -t role aslib.infra.node_image`.

**The deploy** (`deploy.yml`) works as root (through sudo) and, on each server:

1. creates the account `{{ __node_setup_service }}` the service runs as, and lets it run
   services while nobody is logged in (`loginctl enable-linger`);
2. copies the image to `/opt/containers/images/{{ __node_setup_service }}/` and loads it
   into that account's Podman;
3. installs `container.env` and the Quadlet file `{{ __node_setup_service }}.container` in
   `/etc/containers/systemd/users/<the account's UID>/`;
4. creates `/opt/{{ __node_setup_service }}/`, the service's directory, and `data/` in it
   when missing, and copies the config files to its `config/` (removing files the
   project no longer has);
5. starts the service, or restarts it when something changed, and waits until it
   answers on its port (`127.0.0.1:3000` by default). If it does not, it shows
   the service's log and stops.

`/opt/{{ __node_setup_service }}/` is the app's working directory, at the same path in the
container, so relative paths end up there: `config/db_config.json`, `prod.db`,
`data/uploads/`. It belongs to the app; the deploy only manages `config/` in it,
which the app can read but not change. The code stays read-only in the image.
`/tmp` (`os.tmpdir()`) is in memory, at most `podman_service_tmp_size` (512 MB), and
emptied on every restart; for large temporary files the app uses a directory of
its own in its working directory. The service starts again after a reboot, and
after a crash every 10 seconds.

What a server needs: RHEL 9.2 or newer with Podman (`sudo dnf install podman`),
and SSH access with sudo (or as root) for Ansible. Nothing is downloaded: the
image comes from this directory.

**A new version:** raise the `version` in package.json and run
`ansible-playbook build-and-deploy.yml` again. The deploy loads the new image,
restarts the service and waits until it answers. It keeps only the version it
deployed: the archives of other versions and the account's other images are
removed. To go back, check out the previous version of the project and deploy that.

## 4. Check on it

```sh
ansible-playbook aslib.infra.podman_overview      # add --ask-become-pass if sudo asks for a password
```

Shows every service on the servers in `inventory.yml`, this one and others: its
account, state, image and ports, and the latest log lines of each service that
does not run. It changes nothing. On a server itself, the same command from any
directory shows that server, or by hand:

```sh
sudo systemctl --user -M {{ __node_setup_service }}@ status {{ __node_setup_service }}
sudo journalctl _SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT={{ __node_setup_service }}.service   # its output; -f to follow
curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/
```

## 5. Update aslib.infra

Install the newer release, then run:

```sh
ansible-playbook update-playbooks.yml
```

It brings aslib.infra's files up to date, keeping each changed one as
`<file>.bak`, and adds files this directory does not have yet. New settings
appear in `group_vars/all/defaults.yml`; copy one into `project.yml` to change it.
Review the changes before committing them.

## 6. Remove it

```sh
ansible-playbook remove.yml                                     # keeps the app's data
ansible-playbook remove.yml -e podman_service_remove_data=true  # removes it as well
```

This removes what the deploy created on each server: the service, its account
(with its home directory and images), the Quadlet and environment files, the
image archives and the config files. `/opt/{{ __node_setup_service }}/`, with everything
the app wrote, stays unless you ask; a later deploy hands it to the new account.

## Download sources

`prebuild.yml` and `image.yml` download; `deploy.yml` does not. The project
holds what is the same everywhere, each machine what differs:

| What | Set in the project | Set on the machine that builds |
|---|---|---|
| Red Hat's Node.js images, from `registry.access.redhat.com` | `node_image_registry` | `/etc/containers/registries.conf` (or `~/.config/containers/registries.conf`) |
| Everything npm downloads: packages, Node.js headers for native modules, prebuilt binaries | `.npmrc` (wins) | `~/.npmrc` of the user who runs the playbook |
| RPMs in `node_image_build_packages` and `node_image_runtime_packages`, if any | | Red Hat's UBI repositories, as set up in the base images |

Offline checklist for a build machine:

- Images: point Red Hat's registry at your mirror in `registries.conf`:

  ```toml
  [[registry]]
  prefix = "registry.access.redhat.com"
  location = "mirror.example.com/redhat"
  ```

- npm: the registry and everything packages download, in the project's
  `.npmrc` or the machine's `~/.npmrc`, for example:

  ```ini
  registry=http://mirror.example.com/repository/npm/
  disturl=http://mirror.example.com/repository/node/
  sqlite3_binary_host_mirror=http://mirror.example.com/repository/sqlite
  ```

  npm 11 and newer warn that settings it does not know itself (`disturl`,
  `sqlite3_binary_host_mirror`) "will stop working in the next major version";
  they work until then.

## In a pipeline

A pipeline runs the same commands. Keep the steps that run your code apart from
the deploy secrets:

```sh
# Test job: no deploy secrets, no SSH keys.
npm ci && npm run test

# Deploy job, from the ansible/ directory. The runner needs Podman, ansible-core and SSH access.
ansible-galaxy collection install --force <the aslib.infra tarball this project uses>
ansible-playbook image.yml
(umask 077 && printf '%s\n' "$CONTAINER_ENV" > container.env)   # from a secret variable
ansible-playbook deploy.yml
```

`logs/` then holds a log of each run, to keep as the job's artifacts. They never
show `container.env`'s contents.
